  "knownDifficulties": [
    {
      "id": "ambivalent-protective",
      "label": "Ambivalent protective statements",
      "detail": "\"I do not want to die, I just want the pain to stop\" is simultaneously protective and high-risk. The rule layer treats it as protective; in the test set it is labelled as distress, not risk. A clinician would explore it further — the app never treats \"no risk\" as an all-clear."
    },
    {
      "id": "third-party",
      "label": "Third-party risk",
      "detail": "Entries about someone else (\"my cousin said there is no reason to live\") are held at \"watch\" so the writer is not flagged, while support guidance is still offered. The subject classifier is rule-based and will miss reported speech in long paragraphs."
    },
    {
      "id": "figurative",
      "label": "Figurative and cultural language",
      "detail": "Idioms (\"to die for\", \"killing it\", \"when I die…\") are suppressed by an explicit list. Any idiom not on that list can be misread; the suppress list is the most frequently edited file in the engine."
    },
    {
      "id": "sarcasm",
      "label": "Sarcasm and irony",
      "detail": "Inherited from both VADER and lexicon approaches: \"great, another power cut\" reads as positive. Production plans use a fine-tuned transformer with sarcasm-annotated data; the rule engine cannot fix this."
    },
    {
      "id": "labels",
      "label": "Label subjectivity",
      "detail": "The in-house eval labels were written by one annotator. Mood bands are judgement calls at the boundary. A real validation needs at least two annotators per item, a disagreement adjudication step, and Ghanaian clinicians on the panel."
    }
  ]
